; Under the hood — what the optimizer does, and where it stops
; A guided read of the Cranelift IR the compiler actually emits. Each step
; defines a tiny function and shows its /clif — the real IR, no filtering.
; Follow scalar loops, heap records, vector updates and owned arguments.

; Everything here is free-standing on primitives — bare ops, no prelude sugar —
; so the IR isolates exactly the optimization on display.

(import [primitives [*]])


; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; SCALAR LOOPS AND HEAP VALUES
; ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

; 1. Scalars in a loop live in registers, not memory.
; Cranelisp has no loop form — iteration IS tail-self-recursion. A tail call
; lowers to a jump, and the loop-carried values ride the block parameters:

(defn sumto [:Int n :Int acc] (if (eq-i64 n 0) acc (sumto (sub-i64 n 1) (add-i64 acc n))))
/clif sumto

; Read the IR: n and acc ride the loop header's block parameters in registers.
; The recursive tail jumps back to that header without a call or a new frame.
; No allocation appears in this function.


; 2. A single-field record has a heap representation.
; Build a wrapper and immediately match it to read its scalar field:

(deftype Cell (MkCell [:Int v]))
(defn unwrap [:Int n] (let [c (MkCell n)] (match c [(MkCell v) v])))
/clif unwrap

; Read the IR: the constructor calls the allocator and stores its field.
; Matching loads that field and releases the record before returning the Int.


; 3. Mutation happens in place when the value is unique.
; A vec write does not always copy. The compiler emits BOTH paths and picks at
; runtime on the refcount: rc==1 (unique) mutates in place; shared copies first
; (copy-on-write). A freshly built vec is unique, so this takes the fast arm:

(defn set-first [:Int n] (let [v (vec 1 2 3)] (vec-set v 0 n)))
/clif set-first

; Read the IR: after the bounds check, a load at header offset +8 fetches
; the refcount. A comparison with 1 forks to an in-place store or a call to
; the copy-on-write fallback. Uniqueness is tested at runtime.


; 4. Reading an owned value still consumes it.
; Uniform counting passes this Box as an owned argument. The callee reads
; the scalar fields, then releases the Box before returning the selected Int:

(deftype Box (MkBox [:Int a :Int b]))
(defn get-h [:Box x] (match x [(MkBox a b) b]))
/clif get-h

; Read the IR: field loads at +24 and +32 precede a typed release call on
; the Box pointer. The selected scalar then returns. No inline atomic_rmw
; appears here; release work lives behind the call, so its absence is not
; evidence that reading a heap value has no reference-count cost.


; 5. A two-field record follows the same heap path.
; Both fields are scalar, and the record stays within this function:

(deftype Pt (MkPt [:Int x :Int y]))
(defn pair-sum [:Int n] (let [p (MkPt n (add-i64 n 1))] (match p [(MkPt x y) (add-i64 x y)])))
/clif pair-sum

; Read the IR: allocation, field stores, field loads, and a typed release.
; Both Cell and Pt are heap records. Neither becomes a stack record or a set
; of scalar block parameters merely because it is local to this function.

; The emitted IR exposes where work happens: tail calls become loop jumps,
; vector uniqueness selects an update path, and owned records are released
; after their fields are read. Calls can carry allocation and release work
; that would otherwise be easy to miss when reading only inline instructions.
